{
  "chapters": [
    {
      "description": "Write and run your first lines of code in the browser console, in an HTML page, and in the Node REPL, and read the errors that come back.",
      "title": "Running your first JavaScript"
    },
    {
      "description": "Store data with let and const, tell apart strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, and objects, and check types with typeof.",
      "title": "Values, variables, and data types"
    },
    {
      "description": "Arithmetic, comparison, and logical operators, plus the coercion rules behind ==, ===, truthy and falsy values, and the nullish and optional chaining operators.",
      "title": "Operators, expressions, and type coercion"
    },
    {
      "description": "Floating-point precision, rounding, parsing numeric input, the Math object, and when to reach for BigInt in money and ID calculations.",
      "title": "Numbers, Math, and BigInt"
    },
    {
      "description": "Build and transform text with template literals, slicing, searching, splitting, joining, padding, and Unicode-safe handling of characters.",
      "title": "Strings and template literals"
    },
    {
      "description": "Branch your programs with if/else, switch, ternaries, and guard clauses, and write conditions that stay readable as rules pile up.",
      "title": "Conditionals and control flow"
    },
    {
      "description": "Repeat work with for, while, for...of, and for...in, control loops with break and continue, and pick the right loop for each job.",
      "title": "Loops and iteration"
    },
    {
      "description": "Declare functions, arrow functions, and function expressions, use default and rest parameters, and split a script into small reusable pieces.",
      "title": "Functions, parameters, and return values"
    },
    {
      "description": "Create, read, and change lists, then transform data with map, filter, reduce, find, sort, flat, and the newer immutable array methods.",
      "title": "Arrays and array methods"
    },
    {
      "description": "Build objects, nest data, copy versus reference, use computed keys, and work with Object.keys, entries, freeze, and property descriptors.",
      "title": "Objects, properties, and references"
    },
    {
      "description": "How JavaScript resolves names, why var behaves differently from let and const, and how closures let functions remember data after they return.",
      "title": "Scope, hoisting, and closures"
    },
    {
      "description": "What this points to in methods, standalone functions, arrow functions, and callbacks, and how to fix binding with call, apply, and bind.",
      "title": "this, call, apply, and bind"
    },
    {
      "description": "How objects inherit behavior through the prototype chain, and how to use Object.create, prototype lookups, and property shadowing on purpose.",
      "title": "Prototypes and the prototype chain"
    },
    {
      "description": "Write classes with constructors, methods, getters, static members, private fields, and inheritance, and know when a plain object is the better choice.",
      "title": "Classes and object-oriented JavaScript"
    },
    {
      "description": "Pull values out of arrays and objects, spread and merge data, gather arguments with rest, and write cleaner function signatures with these patterns.",
      "title": "Destructuring, spread, and rest"
    },
    {
      "description": "Use Map and Set for keyed and unique collections, choose them over plain objects and arrays, and use symbols and WeakMap for hidden or temporary data.",
      "title": "Map, Set, and symbols"
    },
    {
      "description": "How the iteration protocol works, how to make your own iterable objects, and how generator functions produce values lazily and pause work.",
      "title": "Iterators and generators"
    },
    {
      "description": "Throw and catch errors with try/catch/finally, build custom error classes, and decide where a failure should be handled instead of swallowed.",
      "title": "Errors and exception handling"
    },
    {
      "description": "Match, validate, and extract text with patterns: character classes, groups, anchors, quantifiers, flags, named groups, and the string methods that use them.",
      "title": "Regular expressions"
    },
    {
      "description": "Work with the Date object and the Temporal API, handle time zones and durations, and format dates, numbers, and currencies with Intl.",
      "title": "Dates, times, and internationalization"
    },
    {
      "description": "Convert data to and from JSON with stringify and parse, use replacers and revivers, and handle deep copies and malformed payloads safely.",
      "title": "JSON and data serialization"
    },
    {
      "description": "Higher-order functions, pure functions, immutability, composition, currying, and memoization, applied to real data-transformation tasks.",
      "title": "Functional programming patterns"
    },
    {
      "description": "Split code across files with import and export, understand default versus named exports, dynamic imports, and how CommonJS in Node differs from ES modules.",
      "title": "Modules: ES modules and CommonJS"
    },
    {
      "description": "From Netscape and the browser wars to ES5, ES2015, and yearly releases: how TC39 designs features, how engines like V8 changed performance, and why legacy quirks remain.",
      "title": "JavaScript's evolution and the ECMAScript standard"
    },
    {
      "description": "Select, create, move, and remove elements, change text, attributes, classes, and styles, and build interfaces that respond to data.",
      "title": "The DOM"
    },
    {
      "description": "Listen for clicks, keyboard, and pointer events, use bubbling and delegation, prevent default behavior, and clean up listeners with AbortController.",
      "title": "Events and event handling"
    },
    {
      "description": "Read form fields, work with the FormData API, run constraint and custom validation, show accessible error messages, and submit data without a page reload.",
      "title": "Forms, user input, and validation"
    },
    {
      "description": "Store data in the browser with localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies, and IndexedDB, and pick the right one for preferences, sessions, and offline data.",
      "title": "Client-side storage in the browser"
    },
    {
      "description": "Build reusable custom elements with the Custom Elements API, shadow DOM, slots, and templates, and use them alongside framework code.",
      "title": "Web components and custom elements"
    },
    {
      "description": "How the call stack, task queue, and microtasks decide execution order, plus setTimeout, setInterval, requestAnimationFrame, and callback-based code.",
      "title": "The event loop, timers, and asynchronous execution"
    },
    {
      "description": "Create and chain promises, handle rejection, and coordinate work with Promise.all, allSettled, race, and any.",
      "title": "Promises"
    },
    {
      "description": "Write asynchronous code that reads like normal code, handle errors around await, run tasks in parallel safely, and avoid accidental sequential slowdowns.",
      "title": "async/await and concurrency patterns"
    },
    {
      "description": "Call REST APIs with fetch, send headers and JSON bodies, handle status codes, timeouts, retries, cancellation, CORS, and streaming responses.",
      "title": "Fetch and working with HTTP APIs"
    },
    {
      "description": "Push live updates with WebSockets and server-sent events, handle reconnection and message formats, and build a small chat or live dashboard.",
      "title": "Real-time communication: WebSockets and server-sent events"
    },
    {
      "description": "Run JavaScript outside the browser: modules and the global objects, the file system, paths, streams, buffers, environment variables, processes, and CLI scripts.",
      "title": "Node.js fundamentals"
    },
    {
      "description": "package.json, semantic versioning, lockfiles, scripts, dev versus runtime dependencies, npm/pnpm/yarn differences, workspaces, and publishing your own package.",
      "title": "npm and dependency management"
    },
    {
      "description": "Serve HTTP from Node, then build routes, middleware, request validation, error handling, and JSON APIs with a framework like Express, Fastify, or Hono.",
      "title": "Building HTTP servers and REST APIs"
    },
    {
      "description": "Connect JavaScript apps to SQL and document databases, write queries and migrations, use an ORM such as Prisma or Drizzle, and avoid injection and connection leaks.",
      "title": "Databases and data access from JavaScript"
    },
    {
      "description": "Password hashing, sessions and cookies, JWTs, OAuth and social login, role checks, and protecting both API routes and client-side views.",
      "title": "Authentication and authorization"
    },
    {
      "description": "Set breakpoints, step through code, inspect scope and network traffic, profile memory, read stack traces and source maps, and debug Node processes.",
      "title": "Debugging with DevTools and the Node inspector"
    },
    {
      "description": "Why bundling exists, plus hands-on work with Vite, esbuild, and Rollup: dev servers, hot reload, code splitting, tree shaking, transpiling, and production builds.",
      "title": "Build tools and bundlers"
    },
    {
      "description": "Add static types to JavaScript: annotations, interfaces, unions, generics, narrowing, tsconfig options, typing third-party libraries, and migrating an existing codebase.",
      "title": "TypeScript"
    },
    {
      "description": "Catch mistakes and settle style arguments with ESLint rules, Prettier formatting, editor integration, and pre-commit hooks in a shared project.",
      "title": "Linting, formatting, and code quality"
    },
    {
      "description": "Write and run tests with Vitest or Jest: assertions, test structure, mocks and spies, fake timers, coverage, and testing async and DOM code.",
      "title": "Unit testing JavaScript code"
    },
    {
      "description": "Drive a real browser with Playwright or Cypress, write resilient selectors, test user flows and forms, handle flakiness, and run tests in CI.",
      "title": "End-to-end and browser testing"
    },
    {
      "description": "Build interfaces from components with JSX, props, state, effects, lists, and hooks, and structure a React app that stays maintainable.",
      "title": "Component-based UI with React"
    },
    {
      "description": "How Vue, Svelte, Solid, and Angular track changes with signals and fine-grained reactivity, and how their component models compare to React's.",
      "title": "Reactivity and signals in Vue, Svelte, Solid, and Angular"
    },
    {
      "description": "Move state out of components with stores and reducers, cache server data with query libraries, and decide between local, global, and server state.",
      "title": "State management in front-end apps"
    },
    {
      "description": "Build multi-view apps with a client-side router: routes, params, nested layouts, guards, lazy loading, and the History API underneath.",
      "title": "Single-page apps and client-side routing"
    },
    {
      "description": "Render on the server and hydrate on the client with Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit, or Remix, and work with static generation, streaming, and server components.",
      "title": "Server-side rendering and meta-frameworks"
    },
    {
      "description": "Register a service worker, cache assets and API responses, support offline use, handle background sync and push, and ship an installable web app.",
      "title": "Progressive web apps and service workers"
    },
    {
      "description": "Measure Core Web Vitals, cut bundle size, lazy load, avoid layout thrash and memory leaks, use Web Workers, and profile before and after each fix.",
      "title": "Web performance optimization"
    },
    {
      "description": "Keyboard navigation, focus management, ARIA roles and live regions, accessible modals and menus, and automated plus manual testing with screen readers.",
      "title": "Accessibility in JavaScript interfaces"
    },
    {
      "description": "Defend against XSS, CSRF, clickjacking, prototype pollution, and unsafe redirects with escaping, Content Security Policy, secure cookies, and safe API design.",
      "title": "Web application security"
    },
    {
      "description": "Audit and pin dependencies, read lockfiles, spot typosquatting and malicious packages, use provenance and SBOMs, and keep updates safe with automation.",
      "title": "Software supply chain security in the npm ecosystem"
    },
    {
      "description": "What changed with Deno and Bun: permissions, built-in TypeScript, native test runners and bundlers, standard library choices, and Node compatibility.",
      "title": "Deno and Bun: modern JavaScript runtimes"
    },
    {
      "description": "Deploy JavaScript to serverless functions and edge runtimes, work within cold starts and request limits, use Web-standard APIs, and manage secrets and regions.",
      "title": "Serverless and edge deployment"
    },
    {
      "description": "Call compiled C, Rust, or Go code from JavaScript, pass memory between them, and decide when Wasm actually beats plain JS for heavy work.",
      "title": "WebAssembly with JavaScript"
    },
    {
      "description": "Work effectively with coding assistants and agent tools: prompting from real context, reviewing generated code, testing suggestions, and spotting insecure or outdated output.",
      "title": "AI-assisted JavaScript development"
    },
    {
      "description": "Build product features on model APIs from JavaScript: streaming responses to the UI, tool calling, embeddings and vector search for retrieval, cost control, and evaluating output.",
      "title": "Building AI features with LLM APIs in JavaScript"
    },
    {
      "description": "Follow one feature from ticket and scoping through branching, code review, CI pipelines, staged rollout, logging and error monitoring, and the bug-fix loop after release.",
      "title": "End-to-end project workflow: from ticket to production"
    },
    {
      "description": "Front-end, back-end, full-stack, and specialized paths, portfolio projects and open-source contributions that prove skill, interview preparation, and how developers keep up with the ecosystem.",
      "title": "Career paths in JavaScript development"
    }
  ]
